    "text": "<strong>Paragraph:</strong>\nWave property of electrons implies that they will show diffraction effects. Davisson and Germer demonstrated this by diffracting electrons from crystals. The law governing the diffraction from a crystal is obtained by requiring that electron waves reflected from the planes of atoms in a crystal interfere constructively (see in figure).<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/tDXzo6k7R_tTpNA_mOrnSC8XPn3B0l4WIu-P1D-E49U.original.fullsize.png\"><br><strong>Question:</strong>\nIn an experiment, electrons are made to pass through a narrow slit of width 'd' comparable to their de Broglie wavelength. They are detected on a screen at a distance ' $D$ ' from the slit (see figure).<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/UC8DFWIp82fUv2xMCJp6uBg3SKNxIL6xTtH_-jhH-SM.original.fullsize.png\"><br>\nWhich of the following graph can be expected to represent the number of electrons ' $N$ ' detected as a function of the detector position ' $y$ ' $(y=0$ corresponds to the middle of the slit)?",
    "image": null
  },
